# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
augustine-2.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 2 ways: augustine-2.logic.r1 and augustine-2.logic.r1.3 have "bodies_translated" and "bodies_translated → possessed_cured"; augustine-2.logic.r1.2 has "bodies_translated" and "bodies_translated → possessed_cured ∧ blind_healed"; they differ when only "the bodies were discovered, dug up and translated with due honour to the Ambrosian Basilica" and "those vexed with unclean spirits were cured" are true, which the first allows
augustine-2.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered yes, which keeps augustine-2.logic.r1 and augustine-2.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "the bodies were discovered, dug up and translated with due honour to the Ambrosian Basilica" and "those vexed with unclean spirits were cured" are true and "the blind man touched the bier with his handkerchief and his eyes were opened" is false. Is S2, "For when they were discovered and dug up, and with due honour translated to the Ambrosian Basilica, not only they who were vexed with unclean spirits (the devils confessing themselves) were cured, but a certain man who had for many years been blind, a citizen, and well known to the city, asking and hearing the reason of the people's confused joy, sprang forth desiring his guide to lead him thither.", true then?
augustine-2.md:1: argue/readings: S4 is read 2 ways: augustine-2.logic.r1 and augustine-2.logic.r1.2 have "blind_healed"; augustine-2.logic.r1.3 has "bodies_translated → blind_healed"; they differ when nothing is true, which the second allows
augustine-2.md:1: argue/answered: S4: answered no, which keeps augustine-2.logic.r1 and augustine-2.logic.r1.2, to: Suppose "the bodies were discovered, dug up and translated with due honour to the Ambrosian Basilica" and "the blind man touched the bier with his handkerchief and his eyes were opened" are false. Is S4, "Which when he had done, and put to his eyes, they were forthwith opened.", true then?
augustine-2.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 2 ways: augustine-2.logic.r1 has "possessed_cured ∧ blind_healed → fame_spread" and "fame_spread → empress_turned ∧ ¬empress_converted"; augustine-2.logic.r1.2 and augustine-2.logic.r1.3 have "possessed_cured ∧ blind_healed → fame_spread ∧ empress_turned" and "¬empress_converted"; they differ when only "those vexed with unclean spirits were cured", "the mind of that enemy, the Empress, was turned back from her fury of persecuting" and "the Empress was turned to the soundness of believing" are true, which the first allows
augustine-2.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered no, which keeps augustine-2.logic.r1.2 and augustine-2.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "those vexed with unclean spirits were cured", "the mind of that enemy, the Empress, was turned back from her fury of persecuting" and "the Empress was turned to the soundness of believing" are true and "the blind man touched the bier with his handkerchief and his eyes were opened" and "the fame spread and God's praises glowed" are false. Is S5, "Thence did the fame spread, thence Thy praises glowed, shone; thence the mind of that enemy, though not turned to the soundness of believing, was yet turned back from her fury of persecuting.", true then?
augustine-2.md:1: argue/readings: S9 is read 2 ways: augustine-2.logic.r1 and augustine-2.logic.r1.3 have nothing from it; augustine-2.logic.r1.2 has "odour_fragrant ∧ ¬ran_after → wept_more"; they differ when only "the odour of God's ointments was so fragrant" is true, which the first allows
augustine-2.md:1: argue/answered: S9: answered no, which keeps augustine-2.logic.r1.2, to: Suppose "the odour of God's ointments was so fragrant" is true and "we ran after God" and "I wept more among the singing of God's hymns" are false. Is S9, "Therefore did I more weep among the singing of Thy Hymns, formerly sighing after Thee, and at length breathing in Thee, as far as the breath may enter into this our house of grass.", true then?
augustine-2.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: augustine-2.logic.r1 and augustine-2.logic.r1.3 have "odour_fragrant ∧ ¬ran_after → wept_more"; augustine-2.logic.r1.2 has nothing from it; they differ when only "the odour of God's ointments was so fragrant" is true, which the second allows
